{"name":"Silica Sand","hex":"#ede2e0","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silica","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Silicon dioxide. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Any of the silica group of the silicate minerals."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silica"},{"term":"sand","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Rock that is ground more finely than gravel, but is not as fine as silt (more formally, see grain sizes chart), forming beaches and deserts and also used in construction.\n\n A specific grade, type, or composition of sand.\n\n A beach or other mass of sand.","A specific grade, type, or composition of sand."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a light beige colour, like that of typical sand."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sand"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sand casting","blocks":[{"text":"Sand casting, also known as sand molded casting, is a metal casting process characterized by using sand—known as casting sand—as the mold material. The term \"sand casting\" can also refer to an object produced via the sand casting process. Sand castings are produced in specialized factories called foundries. In 2003, over 60% of all metal castings were produced via sand casting."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/SandMoldCopeDragCores.jpg/330px-SandMoldCopeDragCores.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sand_casting","wikidata":"Q5980103"},{"title":"Silicon dioxide","blocks":[{"text":"Silicon dioxide, also known as silica, is an oxide of silicon with the chemical formula SiO2, and is commonly found in nature as quartz. In many parts of the world, silica is the major constituent of sand. Silica is one of the most complex and abundant families of materials, existing as a compound of several minerals and as a synthetic product. Examples include fused quartz, fumed silica, opal, and aerogels. It is used in structural materials, microelectronics, and as components in the food and pharmaceutical industries. All forms are white or colorless, although impure samples can be colored or opaque."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Sample_of_silicon_dioxide.jpg/330px-Sample_of_silicon_dioxide.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silicon_dioxide","wikidata":"Q116269"},{"title":"Sand","blocks":[{"text":"Sand is a granular material composed of finely divided mineral particles. Sand has various compositions but is usually defined by its grain size. Sand grains are smaller than gravel and coarser than silt. Sand can also refer to a textural class of soil or soil type; i.e., a soil containing more than 85 percent sand-sized particles by mass."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Libya_4608_Idehan_Ubari_Dunes_Luca_Galuzzi_2007.jpg/330px-Libya_4608_Idehan_Ubari_Dunes_Luca_Galuzzi_2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sand","wikidata":"Q34679"},{"title":"Desert sand (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Desert sand is a very light and very weakly saturated reddish yellow color which corresponds specifically to the coloration of sand. It may also be regarded as a deep tone of beige."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Sossusvlei_Dune_Namib_Desert_Namibia_Luca_Galuzzi_2004.JPG/330px-Sossusvlei_Dune_Namib_Desert_Namibia_Luca_Galuzzi_2004.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Desert_sand_(color)","wikidata":"Q9303163"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/SandMoldBronzeAluminium.jpg/500px-SandMoldBronzeAluminium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/SandMoldBronzeAluminium.jpg/1280px-SandMoldBronzeAluminium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/SandMoldBronzeAluminium.jpg/500px-SandMoldBronzeAluminium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Two sets of raw castings (bronze and aluminium), demonstrating the characteristic granular surface texture before finishing. In contemporary design, this \"sand skin\" is often preserved for its aesthetic value.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASandMoldBronzeAluminium.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Aluminium_melting_crucible_casting_process.jpg/500px-Aluminium_melting_crucible_casting_process.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Aluminium_melting_crucible_casting_process.jpg/1280px-Aluminium_melting_crucible_casting_process.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Aluminium_melting_crucible_casting_process.jpg/500px-Aluminium_melting_crucible_casting_process.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Molten metal in a crucible poured into sand form, with surface oxidation and residue typical of the casting process.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAluminium_melting_crucible_casting_process.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Haandform-e.png/500px-Haandform-e.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Haandform-e.png/500px-Haandform-e.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHaandform-e.p